ves, risks |\n| `envelope-fields.md` | 19 top-level fields, 15 `$defs`, JSON Schema encoding per field, assembly order |\n| `fixture-corpus.md` | 16 valid + 23 invalid fixtures, manifest design, base document |\n| `assumptions.md` | A0–A12 with blast radius + falsifier, reconciliation checklist |\n| `traceability.md` | Field→clause→fixture, invariant→fixture, L2 deferrals, acceptance-command mapping |\n\n## The finding that shapes everything\n\n**§4.2's text was not readable from this session.** `constitution/README.md:6-10` says the PAEOS corpus \"currently lives across `derivation/`, `spec/`, and `reviews/`\" and consolidation is a later (B0.3) task. My read scope was `constitution/`, `spec/`, `design/` — only `constitution/` exists, holding a skeleton README and two canaries. Nothing names a node envelope, a field list, or a §4.2.\n\nSo the field roster is **reconstructed** from the three invariant violations the goal itself names, and I've marked it as such rather than presenting it as read. `assumptions.md` gives each assumption a falsifier and a reconciliation step; `design.md` §7 adds a gate requiring either that the checklist be walked, or that the schema carry an `UNRECONCILED` `$comment`. The goal is still executable without §4.2 — the structure (layering, tombstone topology, derived-confidence discipline) survives field renames; only names change.\n\n## Design decisions worth your attention\n\n**Two validation layers, published explicitly.** JSON Schema can't resolve refs, compare values, or recompute hashes. Rather than let the schema silently under-enforce, `traceability.md` §3 names all 10 L2 invariants and assigns each a successor goal. The blunt version: L1 proves confidence *was derived*, not that it was derived *correctly*.\n\n**Transition legality is L1.** Since the envelope carries `promotion.from` and `to` in one document, the 19 legal edges encode as an `anyOf` of const pairs — pulling the whole state machine, including the absorbing `RETRACTED